a  number  of  ‘:’‐separated  fields.   The  first entry for each
record gives the names that are known for the  record,  separated
by  ‘|’  characters.   By  convention, the last name is usually a
comment and is not intended as a lookup tag.  The entry  must  be
terminated  by  the ‘:’ character.  The following entry describes
the  Teletype  model  33.   T3|tty33|33|tty|Teletype  model  33:\
        :bl=ˆG:co#72:.cr=9ˆM:cr=ˆM:do=ˆJ:hc:os:am@:  Entries  may
continue onto multiple lines by giving a \ as the last  character
of a line, and empty fields may be included for readability (here
between  the  last  field  on  a  line and the first field on the
next).  Comments may be included on lines beginning with Capabil‐
ities in are of three types: Boolean capabilities, numeric  capa‐
bilities, and string capabilities.  Boolean capabilities are just
the  name, to indicate the ability is present.  Numeric capabili‐
ties are followed by the character ‘#’ then the  value.   In  the
example above gives the value ‘72’.  String capabilities are fol‐
lowed  by  the character ‘=’ and then the string.  In the example
above gives the value ‘ˆG’.   Sometimes  individual  capabilities
must be commented out.  To do this, put a period (‘.’) before the
capability  name.   For  example,  see  the  first in the example
above.  Sometimes individual capabilities must be marked  as  ab‐
sent.   To do this, put a @ after the capability name.  For exam‐
ple, see the last in the example above.  This is only useful when
merging entries.  See the tc=name discussion below for  more  de‐
tails.   Numeric  capability  values may be given in one of three
numeric bases.  If the number starts with either or it is  inter‐
preted as a hexadecimal number (both upper and lower case a‐f may
be  used  to denote the extended hexadecimal digits).  Otherwise,
if the number starts with a it is interpreted as an octal number.
Otherwise the number is interpreted as a decimal number.   String
